    "reasoning": "All supplied worked solutions explicitly state their answers. Fifteen selections are mathematically confirmed; q-93343 is underdetermined unless its three lateral faces are assumed to have equal areas. Its best supported intended selection is 44 square inches, matching MA, but the unmarked drawing does not establish that equality.\n\nq-114190: The antiderivative is 2z^3-z^5; evaluating from -2 to 1 gives -15. q-29780: Evaluating (4/5)x^(5/2)+(3/2)x^2+x gives 268/5. The source calls this a 'difference' of integrals, although its displayed sum and answer are correct. q-24574: cos(2t)=2cos^2(t)-1 gives y=x^2/4-2, equivalent to option 0. The actual parametric trace additionally requires -4≤x≤4, a restriction omitted from MA's answer. q-61907: Six square faces give 6·25=150 square centimetres. q-93343: MA assumes three side faces of area 13, giving 5+3·13=44 square inches; only one side area is explicitly supplied.\n\nq-88458: The exponential factor is positive, leaving (x-3)(x-4)=0; the product is 12. q-93099: The principal sixth root is nonnegative, so the function is at most -8 and has no real root. q-114007: Equal integration limits give zero. q-114135: The integral of f is 8+4=12, so the requested integral is 5·3-2·12=-9. q-114143: Reversing limits and dividing by 3 gives an integral from 5 to 8 of 2/3; adding -3 gives -7/3. q-4790: The integral from 0 to 3 is 3, so the total is 3+6=9.\n\nq-10978: The shaded positive branch ends at x=1; integrating from 0 to 1 gives -2/3-1/2+3=11/6. q-73333: Geometric area is the integral of the positive magnitude, giving arcsin(1/2)-arcsin(-1/2)=π/3. MA initially labels the signed integral as area, but its final correction is correct. q-13189: Substitution gives (1/3)ln(8/5)=ln(2)-(1/3)ln(5), exactly the selected form. q-73432: Substitution gives (1/4)ln(25/9)=(1/2)ln(5/3). q-121237: For nonzero x, 2x^2 is positive, so both upper entries are positive and both lower entries are negative. No earlier submitted answers or grades were supplied; all MA evidence below comes from each question's own worked solution.",
